nvk-surface-keep: a caller may keep its import of a surface's video memory for longer than one frame (3.0.7).
NullMoth's own change (not an upstream Mesa commit).

The Zink frontend presents by copying its frame into the video memory of the drawable's IOSurface. Through 3.0.6 it
imported that memory again for every frame (a grant from the accelerator, a duplicate of the RM object, a GPU address
range bound and unbound): 6 to 8 % of the thread that swaps in a Minecraft modpack, for a CAMetalLayer that hands out
the same three surfaces in turn. To keep an import, the caller has to learn when the surface no longer has the block
it imported, because a mark (nvmtl_nvk_surface_dirty) names only the surface and would then be taken for memory the
caller never wrote.

The kernel's grant now answers with a name for the block it shared, in the `pad` word of the escape (NVRM.kext 3.0.7,
kexts/NVRM/nvrm_surfshare_abi.h; an older kernel copies the word back as it was sent, 0). Two exports give that to a
caller, which finds them by name and keeps no import when either is missing:

  nvmtl_nvk_import_cookie()            the name the last import on this thread received. It is cleared when an import
                                       starts and set only when the import has succeeded, so a caller that reads it
                                       right after vkAllocateMemory returned VK_SUCCESS reads the name of the memory
                                       it holds, and 0 when the kernel did not tell.
  nvmtl_nvk_surface_dirty_at(…cookie)  the mark of nvmtl_nvk_surface_dirty with that name in `pad`: the kernel takes
                                       it only if the surface still has that block and answers 0x8000000A
                                       (NVRM_SS_MOVED) otherwise, with nothing marked.

Nothing that exists changes: nvmtl_nvk_stage_import, nvmtl_nvk_surface_dirty and the import itself send and do what
they did, and a caller that does not ask for the name is not told. What the import relies on is unchanged too: the
duplicate of the RM object keeps the memory alive in this client for as long as the VkDeviceMemory lives, whatever
the surface does with its own handle meanwhile (the Metal plugin's imports have always relied on that).

diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_device_memory.c b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_device_memory.c
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_device_memory.c
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_device_memory.c
@@ -138,6 +138,17 @@
    return nvkmd_nvrm_surface_dirty(dev->nvkmd, surfaceID, plane);
 }
 
+/* nvmtl_nvk_surface_dirty for an import that is kept: see nvkmd_nvrm_surface_dirty_at. A caller looks this up by name
+ * together with nvmtl_nvk_import_cookie, and keeps no import when either is missing. */
+__attribute__((visibility("default"))) uint32_t   /* exported: the Zink frontend's dlsym */
+nvmtl_nvk_surface_dirty_at(VkDevice device, uint32_t surfaceID, uint32_t plane, uint32_t cookie)
+{
+   VK_FROM_HANDLE(nvk_device, dev, device);
+   if (dev == NULL || dev->nvkmd == NULL)
+      return 0x80000002u;   /* NVRM_SS_OFF */
+   return nvkmd_nvrm_surface_dirty_at(dev->nvkmd, surfaceID, plane, cookie);
+}
+
 /* The surface fence: see nvkmd_nvrm_fence. The plugin finds this by name and calls it through its own prototype, so the
  * signature never changes; a new one gets a new name. */
 __attribute__((visibility("default"))) uint32_t   /* exported: plugin dlsym */
